WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.879
This briefing comes from a companion guide
to the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:09.512
and it's about something every
program does every week: meet.

4
00:00:09.512 --> 00:00:11.262
The guide starts from one idea.

5
00:00:11.262 --> 00:00:14.412
A meeting is a tool for
deciding and coordinating.

6
00:00:14.412 --> 00:00:16.872
If it ends without decisions and owners,

7
00:00:16.872 --> 00:00:20.375
it was a status report that cost
an hour of everyone's time.

8
00:00:20.375 --> 00:00:24.682
I'll follow the same four questions as
the book decks: why meetings matter,

9
00:00:24.682 --> 00:00:28.918
what a good meeting needs, who does what
in the room, and how to run one from

10
00:00:28.918 --> 00:00:33.512
before it starts to after it ends, with
two templates and a checklist at the end.

11
00:00:33.512 --> 00:00:37.160
Let's start with why this matters
to a modernization program.

12
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.185
Why should a modernization
program care about meeting habits?

13
00:00:44.185 --> 00:00:47.585
Because meetings are where the
program is actually coordinated.

14
00:00:47.585 --> 00:00:52.071
From the change advisory board to the
cutover go or no-go call to the steering

15
00:00:52.071 --> 00:00:56.485
committee, most of the program's decisions
get made in a meeting of some kind.

16
00:00:56.485 --> 00:01:00.162
And the guide makes a sharper
point: most of a program's slippage

17
00:01:00.162 --> 00:01:04.335
starts in a meeting that ended without
a clear decision or a named owner.

18
00:01:04.335 --> 00:01:07.260
So a meeting that drifts
isn't just a lost hour.

19
00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.898
It can be the moment a schedule starts to
slip, with nobody holding the next step.

20
00:01:11.898 --> 00:01:14.660
The good news is that the fix isn't new.

21
00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:22.685
These habits have a long track record,
and that's the best reason to trust them.

22
00:01:22.685 --> 00:01:27.485
At Intel, a course on effective meetings
was mandatory for new employees,

23
00:01:27.485 --> 00:01:31.085
and Andy Grove often taught it
himself while he was C E O.

24
00:01:31.085 --> 00:01:35.593
In government, G S A's eighteen F,
the federal digital-services team,

25
00:01:35.593 --> 00:01:39.601
published a short public guide
built on the same few ideas:

26
00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:44.610
a clear purpose, a complete agenda,
assigned roles and written next steps.

27
00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.057
And university faculty-development and

28
00:01:47.057 --> 00:01:49.935
engineering programs
teach the same structure.

29
00:01:49.935 --> 00:01:54.299
When industry, government and
academia keep landing on one pattern,

30
00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:56.247
it's because the pattern works.

31
00:01:56.247 --> 00:01:58.280
Here it is in a single picture.

32
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.379
It helps to see the whole routine at once,

33
00:02:04.379 --> 00:02:07.155
because each phase depends
on the one before it.

34
00:02:07.155 --> 00:02:10.195
Before the meeting, you
set it up: the purpose,

35
00:02:10.195 --> 00:02:13.067
the agenda, the roles
and the right people.

36
00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:18.805
During it, you run it: on time, on the
agenda, with anything off-topic parked.

37
00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:22.458
After it, you follow through:
written notes the same day,

38
00:02:22.458 --> 00:02:25.380
and every action with an
owner and a due date.

39
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.655
The part to notice is the arrow back.

40
00:02:27.655 --> 00:02:32.705
What comes out of one meeting opens the next
one's agenda, so nothing quietly drops.

41
00:02:32.705 --> 00:02:37.180
But there's a question that comes before
all of this: do you need a meeting at all?

42
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.322
Every meeting spends its attendees' time,

43
00:02:43.322 --> 00:02:45.855
so the first question
is whether to hold one.

44
00:02:45.855 --> 00:02:47.527
Eighteen F's test is simple:

45
00:02:47.527 --> 00:02:51.480
is this meeting a better use of
everyone's time than not meeting?

46
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.452
A faculty-development session
at a children's hospital

47
00:02:54.452 --> 00:02:57.618
in Pittsburgh looked at why
people actually call meetings.

48
00:02:57.618 --> 00:03:01.793
Some reasons are good ones, like giving
information or working a problem.

49
00:03:01.793 --> 00:03:06.968
Others are more honest about habit:
satisfying requirements, or simply tradition.

50
00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:10.804
Its advice is to ask whether some of
those goals could be met another way.

51
00:03:10.804 --> 00:03:14.760
The guide turns that question
into a simple working rule.

52
00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.910
This rule matters because it gives
people permission not to meet.

53
00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.735
It's the guide's own design guidance for
the program, built on those two sources.

54
00:03:26.735 --> 00:03:30.648
If information only needs to flow
one way, send a written update,

55
00:03:30.648 --> 00:03:34.335
and meet only if the questions
really need live discussion.

56
00:03:34.335 --> 00:03:37.255
If there's a problem to
work or a decision to make,

57
00:03:37.255 --> 00:03:41.447
that's what meetings are for, so meet,
with the right people in the room.

58
00:03:41.447 --> 00:03:44.573
And give every recurring
meeting a check once a quarter.

59
00:03:44.573 --> 00:03:47.993
One that no longer decides
anything should be cancelled,

60
00:03:47.993 --> 00:03:49.885
or turned into a written report.

61
00:03:49.885 --> 00:03:54.160
Once you've decided to meet, the next
question is what kind of meeting it is.

62
00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.747
Knowing the kind of meeting
tells people how to take part.

63
00:04:00.747 --> 00:04:04.084
Engineering guidance from
Portland State University,

64
00:04:04.084 --> 00:04:09.087
drawing on Cameron Herold's book Meetings
Suck, sorts meeting work into three types:

65
00:04:09.087 --> 00:04:12.785
sharing information, solving a
problem, and making a decision.

66
00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:14.510
Each one needs something different.

67
00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.548
Sharing works when the
material goes out in advance.

68
00:04:17.548 --> 00:04:20.700
Problem solving needs a
time limit, a note-taker,

69
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.660
and a phase that opens up ideas
before one that narrows them.

70
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.770
And a decision needs the question,

71
00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:30.010
and the way the group will
decide, agreed beforehand.

72
00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.349
One meeting can hold more than one type,

73
00:04:32.349 --> 00:04:36.735
but each agenda item should be one of
them, and the agenda should say which.

74
00:04:36.735 --> 00:04:39.270
The same source adds two warnings.

75
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.070
These two warnings land
hard on technical programs,

76
00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:49.332
where it's tempting to solve
the problem live, on the call.

77
00:04:49.332 --> 00:04:52.044
The first is: don't do
the work in the meeting.

78
00:04:52.044 --> 00:04:57.148
When the group starts doing detailed analysis
together, stop, agree who will do it,

79
00:04:57.148 --> 00:05:00.957
by when and what they'll deliver,
and then go back to the agenda.

80
00:05:00.957 --> 00:05:03.121
The second is: name the shift.

81
00:05:03.121 --> 00:05:06.971
When the discussion moves from
reporting into problem solving,

82
00:05:06.971 --> 00:05:11.257
or from problem solving into deciding,
the facilitator says so out loud.

83
00:05:11.257 --> 00:05:14.833
It keeps everyone working on the
same task at the same moment.

84
00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:18.930
Both warnings depend on a good
agenda, and that's the next topic.

85
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.318
The agenda matters more
than most people think,

86
00:05:25.318 --> 00:05:29.580
because it defines what the meeting
covers and tells everyone when it's over.

87
00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.542
When the agenda is done,
the meeting is done.

88
00:05:32.542 --> 00:05:34.841
A list of topic names can't do that job;

89
00:05:34.841 --> 00:05:38.980
a line that just says network tells
nobody what's expected of them.

90
00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:43.106
A real agenda writes each item
as a question or an outcome,

91
00:05:43.106 --> 00:05:47.144
says whether it's there to
inform, to discuss or to decide,

92
00:05:47.144 --> 00:05:50.568
and gives it an owner, a
time and any preparation.

93
00:05:50.568 --> 00:05:52.831
Which three pilot sites go first?

94
00:05:52.831 --> 00:05:55.792
focuses a room far
better than pilot sites.

95
00:05:55.792 --> 00:05:59.670
Around those items, a few more
details make the difference.

96
00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.839
These details are small, but
they're what let people prepare,

97
00:06:06.839 --> 00:06:08.933
and decide whether they need to come.

98
00:06:08.933 --> 00:06:12.657
Above the items, state the
purpose and the expected outcomes,

99
00:06:12.657 --> 00:06:14.669
and say where the notes will live.

100
00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:19.604
Give the meeting a specific title,
like cutover go or no-go, wave two,

101
00:06:19.604 --> 00:06:24.082
rather than weekly sync, and keep
the agenda to a single page.

102
00:06:24.082 --> 00:06:27.430
Send it at least a day ahead,
and let people propose changes

103
00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:31.057
before the meeting instead of
debating the agenda at its start.

104
00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:35.169
Eighteen F also suggests fifteen-
or forty-five-minute meetings,

105
00:06:35.169 --> 00:06:39.591
which leave room between meetings
and spare time if an item runs over,

106
00:06:39.591 --> 00:06:41.608
and about a dozen people at most.

107
00:06:41.608 --> 00:06:44.930
With the agenda set, let's
look at who does what.

108
00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.195
Roles matter because the guide's rule is
simple: every meeting needs named roles,

109
00:06:53.195 --> 00:06:56.068
and every attendee should
know which one they hold.

110
00:06:56.068 --> 00:07:00.081
The table shows the six and what
each does, so I'll pick out two.

111
00:07:00.081 --> 00:07:04.000
The facilitator focuses on the
process, not the content, and doesn't

112
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.992
have to be the person who called the
meeting; it's wise to have a backup.

113
00:07:07.992 --> 00:07:10.368
And the decision-maker
has to be in the room.

114
00:07:10.368 --> 00:07:13.036
If the person with the
authority isn't there,

115
00:07:13.036 --> 00:07:17.780
an item marked decide simply can't be
decided, however good the discussion was.

116
00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:22.120
The role that's forgotten most
often, though, is the note-taker.

117
00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.302
Eighteen F calls failing to
assign a note-taker the most

118
00:07:29.302 --> 00:07:32.883
common meeting mistake, and it's
also the easiest one to fix.

119
00:07:32.883 --> 00:07:36.270
Any attendee can simply
ask: who's taking notes?

120
00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:38.445
Roles don't need a crowd, either.

121
00:07:38.445 --> 00:07:42.478
In a small or recurring meeting,
one person can hold two of them,

122
00:07:42.478 --> 00:07:46.670
the facilitator acting as closer,
or the note-taker keeping time.

123
00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:50.244
And rotating the roles across
the team spreads the skill,

124
00:07:50.244 --> 00:07:53.133
so the meeting doesn't
depend on any one person.

125
00:07:53.133 --> 00:07:57.580
With the roles in place, here's how
a meeting runs from start to finish.

126
00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:03.210
Thinking in four phases helps,

127
00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.668
because each one produces
something the next one needs.

128
00:08:06.668 --> 00:08:11.022
The picture earlier showed three; the
guide's routine splits out a fourth,

129
00:08:11.022 --> 00:08:14.868
the close, a few minutes reserved
at the end of the meeting itself.

130
00:08:14.868 --> 00:08:17.545
That's where discussion
turns into decisions

131
00:08:17.545 --> 00:08:20.081
read back and actions
with owners and dates.

132
00:08:20.081 --> 00:08:22.806
Before gives you an agenda
and the right people;

133
00:08:22.806 --> 00:08:27.279
during gives you the discussion and the
decisions; and after puts everything

134
00:08:27.279 --> 00:08:31.193
in writing and carries it into the
next agenda, which closes the loop.

135
00:08:31.193 --> 00:08:35.680
Let's take each phase in turn,
starting before anyone joins.

136
00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:42.605
Much of a meeting's success
is settled before it starts.

137
00:08:42.605 --> 00:08:46.143
First, confirm the meeting is
needed, and what type it is.

138
00:08:46.143 --> 00:08:49.934
Then write the agenda and send it
with the preparation attached,

139
00:08:49.934 --> 00:08:53.368
assign the roles, and invite
only the people the items need.

140
00:08:53.368 --> 00:08:57.808
The children's hospital guidance gives
a good test for that invitation list:

141
00:08:57.808 --> 00:08:59.781
the people affected by a decision,

142
00:08:59.781 --> 00:09:03.868
and the people with the knowledge,
resources or authority it requires.

143
00:09:03.868 --> 00:09:07.499
Shortly before the meeting, send
a reminder with the agenda,

144
00:09:07.499 --> 00:09:10.018
then arrive early and test the equipment.

145
00:09:10.018 --> 00:09:11.940
Now the meeting begins.

146
00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.590
The first few minutes set the
tone for everything after them.

147
00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:20.528
Start on time, every time.

148
00:09:20.528 --> 00:09:24.765
Open by stating the purpose, confirming
the agenda and naming the roles,

149
00:09:24.765 --> 00:09:28.140
and point people to the notes,
slides and chat you're using.

150
00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:31.343
Agree on a few ground
rules, such as take space,

151
00:09:31.343 --> 00:09:33.853
make space, so the air time is shared.

152
00:09:33.853 --> 00:09:39.277
From then on, follow the agenda, and leave
it only deliberately, for a set time.

153
00:09:39.277 --> 00:09:40.664
Summarize as you go.

154
00:09:40.664 --> 00:09:42.331
And when someone arrives late,

155
00:09:42.331 --> 00:09:46.128
point them to the agenda and the
notes rather than recapping for them.

156
00:09:46.128 --> 00:09:48.516
So what happens to the
topics that don't fit?

157
00:09:48.516 --> 00:09:50.760
That's the job of the parking lot.

158
00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:58.872
A parking lot lets you protect the agenda
without dismissing anyone's point.

159
00:09:58.872 --> 00:10:03.354
Topics that are off the agenda, or can't
be settled in the time, go somewhere

160
00:10:03.354 --> 00:10:07.835
everyone can see, like a section of the
notes or a corner of the whiteboard.

161
00:10:07.835 --> 00:10:12.223
Each one gets an action, and the whole
lot is reviewed before the meeting ends.

162
00:10:12.223 --> 00:10:15.797
That follow-up is what keeps people
willing to park a topic at all.

163
00:10:15.797 --> 00:10:17.235
Notes work the same way.

164
00:10:17.235 --> 00:10:19.797
Capture the meaning, not the dialogue,

165
00:10:19.797 --> 00:10:24.673
put decisions and assigned tasks first,
and fill in any gaps the same day.

166
00:10:24.673 --> 00:10:26.390
Then comes the close.

167
00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:34.334
The close is where discussion becomes
commitment, so it gets its own

168
00:10:34.334 --> 00:10:38.527
place on the agenda: the last few
minutes, with the aim of ending early.

169
00:10:38.527 --> 00:10:40.191
Read back the decisions.

170
00:10:40.191 --> 00:10:43.946
Then read back each action item
with its owner and its due date,

171
00:10:43.946 --> 00:10:46.302
and have each owner confirm it out loud.

172
00:10:46.302 --> 00:10:50.537
Route every parking-lot item, ask what's
missing and what to change about the

173
00:10:50.537 --> 00:10:54.977
meeting next time, and set the next meeting
and its facilitator if one is needed.

174
00:10:54.977 --> 00:10:57.320
Then the work moves outside the room.

175
00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:04.557
Follow-through is where a
meeting's value is kept or lost.

176
00:11:04.557 --> 00:11:09.595
Send the notes and action items in writing,
with owners and dates, the same day.

177
00:11:09.595 --> 00:11:12.469
Store them wherever the
team keeps its records,

178
00:11:12.469 --> 00:11:16.007
and make any correction visible
to everyone who attended.

179
00:11:16.007 --> 00:11:19.808
Then open the next meeting by
reviewing the previous action items.

180
00:11:19.808 --> 00:11:22.096
And avoid the meeting after the meeting.

181
00:11:22.096 --> 00:11:25.643
Decisions made in the hallway
leave out the remote attendees,

182
00:11:25.643 --> 00:11:27.382
and they never reach the notes.

183
00:11:27.382 --> 00:11:31.560
To make all of this routine,
the guide gives two templates.

184
00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:39.398
A template matters because it makes
the good agenda the easy one to write.

185
00:11:39.398 --> 00:11:41.436
This is the guide's agenda template.

186
00:11:41.436 --> 00:11:46.454
The top block carries everything we covered
earlier: a specific title, the length,

187
00:11:46.454 --> 00:11:51.023
a one-sentence purpose, the expected
outcomes and where the notes are kept.

188
00:11:51.023 --> 00:11:54.622
Then the roles, by name,
including the decision-maker.

189
00:11:54.622 --> 00:11:59.373
Each item is a question, with its
type, owner, minutes and preparation.

190
00:11:59.373 --> 00:12:02.845
Look at the first and last
rows: every meeting opens by

191
00:12:02.845 --> 00:12:06.948
reviewing the open action items,
and ends with a reserved close.

192
00:12:06.948 --> 00:12:09.420
The second template records what happened.

193
00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:16.525
The notes template is built around
what people need afterward,

194
00:12:16.525 --> 00:12:17.945
and that isn't a transcript.

195
00:12:17.945 --> 00:12:23.195
After a short header with attendees,
absentees and roles, it has four parts.

196
00:12:23.195 --> 00:12:26.120
Decisions, each with who decided and how.

197
00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:30.383
Action items, each with an
owner, a due date and a status.

198
00:12:30.383 --> 00:12:33.157
The parking lot, with
where each item is routed.

199
00:12:33.157 --> 00:12:36.933
And open questions, each with
who will answer, and by when.

200
00:12:36.933 --> 00:12:40.207
It ends by setting the next
meeting and its facilitator.

201
00:12:40.207 --> 00:12:43.850
Everything we've covered
also fits on a single page.

202
00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:52.025
A checklist matters because it puts every
habit in one place you can check quickly.

203
00:12:52.025 --> 00:12:55.702
This one follows the same
moments we've walked through:

204
00:12:55.702 --> 00:12:59.625
before the meeting, the start,
during it, the close, and after.

205
00:12:59.625 --> 00:13:04.157
Each row is a handful of quick checks, from
whether a meeting is needed at all, to

206
00:13:04.157 --> 00:13:05.927
the decision-maker attending,

207
00:13:05.927 --> 00:13:09.113
to the action items being
reviewed at the next meeting.

208
00:13:09.113 --> 00:13:11.287
It's the whole guide, on one page.

209
00:13:11.287 --> 00:13:15.260
For anyone who wants to go deeper,
two books are worth the time.

210
00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:21.747
The two books go deeper
than any guide can.

211
00:13:21.747 --> 00:13:26.173
Andy Grove's High Output Management is
his own account of managing at Intel,

212
00:13:26.173 --> 00:13:28.386
and it includes a chapter on meetings.

213
00:13:28.386 --> 00:13:31.356
Cameron Herold's Meetings
Suck is the source of the

214
00:13:31.356 --> 00:13:34.398
roles and meeting types in
Portland State's toolbox.

215
00:13:34.398 --> 00:13:38.902
But the standard itself fits in one
line: a real agenda, named roles,

216
00:13:38.902 --> 00:13:43.073
a parking lot, and written action
items with owners and dates.

217
00:13:43.073 --> 00:13:45.966
The backup slides list
every source in full,

218
00:13:45.966 --> 00:13:48.860
and the guide itself is
on the workbook site.

219
00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:49.680
Thank you.
